// @check
// @error is `__callsite__`, so its type must be `{ file: string, line: i32, col: i32 }`
// @error argument 2: expected string, got {file: string, line: i32, col: i32}
// The DESTINATION is checked at the DECLARATION. Without this the only error a
// mis-annotated `__callsite__` parameter produces is an argument mismatch at
// somebody else's call site — the declaration is where the mistake is, and a
// library's mistake should not surface first in a consumer's file.
//
// The test is STRUCTURAL, so the track-caller follow-up's `type CallerLoc =
// { file: string, line: i32, col: i32 }` satisfies it with no compiler change:
// an alias resolves to the same object type, and the compiler never learns the
// std name.
//
// The SECOND error is the substitution speaking for itself: the omitted argument
// at `f(1)` is the per-site location object, checked where it LANDS — so the
// call site names the same mistake in the caller's own vocabulary.
function f(a: i32, c: string = __callsite__): string { c }
print(f(1))
